WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:01.990 align:start position:0%
 
all cars go through rigorous handling  tests before being rated safe to drive  but did you know that there is one test  that most cars fail and new cars will  keep failing as well stick around as i  answer that question in this video the  moose test  the test is known as the moose test  developed by technical a swedish  motoring magazine it is a test designed  to simulate the sudden appearance of an  animal on the road unfortunately many  new cars aren't up to the task the test  involves driving at high speeds and  quickly swerving around an obstacle  without braking it measures a car's  cornering ability and resistance to  falling over you basically weave through  a set of cones that replicate swerving  to avoid something on the road like a  moose or in most countries that would be  a pedestrian or a car driving out from a  blind junction you should approach the  cones at around 40 miles per hour and  make the turn in without stepping on the  brakes the test is performed on a dry  surface loaded with people and baggage  to simulate real-life scenarios however  the test is so aggressive that almost  all cars fail even at lower speeds most  cars can pass the test at a speed of  around 10 miles per hour but anything  beyond that you will start to see cars  skidding out of control or even rolling  over completely most cars fail this test  because they're not designed to handle  such sudden movements the test may look  benign on your laptop screens but i can  speak from personal experience that the  amount of steering lock needed to pass  this test properly is scary even for  experienced drivers so why do cars keep  failing the moose test i've done some  digging to find out exactly what's going  on reasons for failing the moose test  became world famous when the 1997  mercedes a-class failed the test in  dramatic style at just under 40 miles  per hour the a-class has a tool body and  a short wheelbase design which is great  for passenger space but it puts the  centre of gravity of the car high a  car's center of gravity matters a lot  because the higher the centre of gravity  the easier it is for the vehicle to roll  over when cornering hard so while  mercedes failed the test a much older  and widely mocked car from former east  germany the trabant managed it perfectly  initially mercedes denied the issue but  eventually admitted that the car had a  huge problem and recalled all of the  a-classes sold unfortunately the recall  was quite expensive costing mercedes an  insane 160 million dollars 25 years on  and cars are still failing the dreaded  moose test there are various reasons for  this but perhaps the most common cause  is simply that manufacturers don't  design their vehicles keeping this test  in mind as a result many car makers  don't even know about the test and even  if they did the cost of redesigning a  car just to pass the moose test would be  too high for most companies i mean think  about it to successfully pass this test  you need to be traveling at a speed of  over 40 miles an hour then you have to  wind the steering in one direction fully  and then quickly unwind it in the  opposite direction this type of  aggressive driving is bound to unsettle  any car no matter how safe the handling  is secondly car manufacturers are under  increasing pressure to meet ever more  stringent emission standards and as a  result they're often forced to use  lighter weight materials in their cars  so they don't have the weight or the  sturdiness to pin them to the ground  during such aggressive maneuvers and to  be honest you will rarely encounter this  type of scenario on the road and it just  isn't considered a prime measure of  safety for car manufacturers primarily  the test is used to find the final  tipping point of new cars rather than  optimizing their designs to pass it  manufacturers would undoubtedly brag  about it if their cars could complete  the test at record speeds but if not  they usually stay silent about it most  moose tests on modern cars are performed  by independent reviewers and automotive  journalists not by manufacturers so what  are some big names that have failed the  moose test spectacularly and are there  any cars that are particularly good at  this test and managed to pass it  successfully failure and successes  although most cars fail the moose test  some designs are inherently conducive to  this type of testing but before we go  into the successes let's look at some of  the failures first a recent prominent  example of failure is the toyota hilux  when it nearly rolled running this test  at the required 40 miles an hour of  course the hilux is an off-road focused  pickup truck so you may say that it  shouldn't be subject to the same sort of  test and you're right but most modern  general suvs also fail this test  spectacularly cars like the jeep grand  cherokee or the porsche mccann seem not  to do well in this test despite their  promise safety and handling  characteristics these suvs are  incredibly heavy and as most of them are  built for off-roading they generally  have a tall ride height which doesn't  help when cornering quickly in addition  their suspension is usually very soft to  aid traction in the mud so in a way if  you were to design a car that wasn't  good for the moose test these would be  your ideal choices a solution to this  could be adding a stiffer anti-roll bar  to the rear however this design change  would impact the off-road ability of the  suvs and trucks so i guess manufacturers  of modern suvs and trucks make a  compromise there but what about sports  cars they should do well on this test  with their superb track handling  characteristics and racing pedigree  right but you'd be surprised that even  sports cars like the bmw m competition  or the mercedes-amg a45 have all been  undone by this brutal test the problem  with these cars is that despite their  highly tuned handling a sudden load is  placed on the rear end during this test  so when the drivers turn in the vehicles  would rotate properly but the reel would  slide around think of a giant pendulum  with all the momentum concentrated at  one end generating a lot of torque in  the opposite direction and spinning it  around however the approach speed and  the sudden aggressive maneuver are  enough to turn them around even with  traction control the other reason these  sports cars fail this test is due to a  lack of braking if the driver was  allowed to brake and turn in as they  would on a typical race track they would  actually place more weight on the front  of the car and generate a lot of  front-end grip to minimize the  understeer and the eventual spin but  since you're supposed to perform this  test without breaking even most sports  cars have no chance to get around the  cone without spinning around however  some cars really excel in this test the  first is the tesla model x an suv  similar to the cars i mentioned earlier  but its heavy battery pack is placed in  just the right place on the floor it's  centered in the middle which means that  despite it being much heavier than some  of its combustion engine competitors it  can complete the test at up to 40 miles  an hour but the all-time great that has  held the record on the moose test for 22  years is not some supercar or even a  small electric hatchback with all its  weight in the middle instead it is the  mighty citroen zantia activa v6 a very  average family saloon from 1999 and it  achieved an incredible speed of 53 miles  per hour this is insane considering most  modern cars fail the test at around 10  miles per hour it could only achieve  this because the zantia has a  computer-controlled active suspension  system that offers roll-free formula one  style cornering like the famous williams  fw15c  this car used a hydraulic suspension  that could adapt to every corner on a  circuit where a standard suspension  would overload the outside wheels during  the aggressive maneuver and lift the  inside wheels the active suspension on  the zantia manages the load on each tyre  individually instantaneously adjusting  the ride height of the car with its  variable shock absorbers the zantia can  change from a soft to a firm suspension  in less than half a second leaning into  the corners and controlling the weight  transfer perfectly therefore pinning the  car to the ground during the aggressive  maneuvers of the test because of this no  other cars have managed to even come  close to zantia's insane performance in  the moose test so if you wish to check  if your family car can pass the moose  test make sure it has an active  suspension system installed if not don't  even try to attempt it as you will most  likely fail and damage your vehicle so  the next time you see a car commercial  bragging about how the vehicle was put  through rigorous safety testing remember  that it probably hasn't been put through  the dreaded moose test the test that  most cars fail and will continue to fail  in the near future thanks for watching  if you enjoyed this video there's more  click a card on the screen and i'll see  you there

